# P&S model routing

Apply the host-neutral role contract in `AGENTS.md`. Keep the manifest and
escalation packet model-neutral so another host can map the same roles.

- `architecture_planner`: user-selected Sol.
- `control_plane_owner`: Sol parent; mandatory for Heavy live work.
- `procedure_coordinator`: optional Luna XHigh procedural assistance under a
  frozen checklist; it cannot own stage transitions or architecture.
- `bounded_implementer`: Luna XHigh.
- routine `independent_tester`: Terra High in a separate read-only context.
- high-risk or cross-contract tester: delegated Sol Medium when the broader
  reasoning capability is justified.
- `escalation_architect`: delegated Sol Medium; use Sol High only in a
  user-selected fresh chat when available.

Before every routine procedural or testing turn, select or verify the assigned
model. Never inherit an expensive parent model accidentally for routine work.
The procedure coordinator follows the frozen checklist and may not redesign
it, expand writable scope, weaken acceptance, own stage transitions, invent
live actions, or override contradictory evidence.

Select Terra High explicitly for the independent reviewer. If the current host
cannot delegate that model, use a user-selected fresh Terra High read-only chat
or report the unavailable assignment; never substitute another model silently.
The reviewer prioritizes concrete defects and acceptance risks over idealized
code. It reviews only the diff under review plus the stage's stated acceptance
criteria, and raises a finding only when the change plausibly causes a concrete
failure (incorrect behavior, safety-envelope violation, acceptance failure,
regression in a touched component, or data/credential loss). It must not request
style-only cleanup, naming/"truthfulness" wording that does not change behavior,
speculative abstractions, public-service or multi-tenant hardening, added
coverage beyond proving the change, or perfection-oriented refactors without a
plausible impact on this private local project. The one recheck confirms only
that the parent-classified prior findings are resolved and the fix added no new
regression; a brand-new item is classified by the parent and does not by itself
authorize another repair. Local deployment does not excuse credential exposure,
unsafe command execution, runtime-input safety, data-loss risks, or other
defects with a concrete local impact. Full contract:
[`docs/flow-delivery-validation-policy.md`](../../docs/flow-delivery-validation-policy.md).

Named agents still require a manifest-compatible assignment. The
`pns-flow-implementer` assignment is Luna XHigh; verify the explicit model on
every mutable implementation or repair turn rather than inheriting the parent.

Use a compact checkpoint packet containing only the manifest, changed paths,
compact diff summary, test receipts, tester findings, and unresolved decisions.
Do not forward the full orchestration transcript. Codex remains portable by
mapping these roles to its available GPT models without requiring Cursor-only
model names or capabilities.

Routine live flow development is convergence-governed by `AGENTS.md` and the
flow-attempt ledger. The Sol control-plane owner owns the CONTINUE /
STEP_BACK_REDESIGN / ESCALATE_USER / STOP decision and repairs local defects
in-session without delegating a fresh Luna implementation or Terra review per
micro-fix. Delegate a bounded Luna XHigh implementer and a Terra High reviewer
only for the STEP_BACK redesign, architecture, cross-contract, or
safety-boundary case, or when `diminishing_returns` mandates a batched
re-derivation. Do not spin up per-defect delegated turns.
